<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Places I Can Go – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Places I Can Go</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm at School.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Outline the meaning of simple paragraphs. Reading: Follow written instructions for making or doing something simple. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify the main idea in simple paragraphs about locations (e.g., "I'm at school."). Can follow written instructions to identify or describe landmarks in a town (e.g., "Find the library on the map." "Point to the park in the picture."). Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable and two-syllable familiar words (e.g., blending the sounds /ɪ-n/ to form the word "in" and the sounds /b-ɪ-g/ to form the word "big").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone!" and displays large flashcards or pictures of places from the previous lesson (school, park, market, library, home). The teacher points to each picture and asks, "Where is this place?" Students respond chorally or individually (e.g., "School!", "Park!"). The teacher then holds up a picture of a school and says, "Today we are at school!" and points to the actual classroom, "I'm at school!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of "school" and "market". The teacher clearly pronounces the words, exaggerating the /sk/ sound in "school" and the /m/ sound in "market". The teacher then writes the words on the board and underlines the target sounds. The teacher models the phrase "I'm at school." and "I'm at the park." using simple hand gestures to indicate location. For phonemic awareness, the teacher says sounds like /ɪ-n/ and blends them into "in", then /b-ɪ-g/ into "big", asking students to repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple questions:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I at home or at school?" (Teacher points to the classroom.) Expected answer: "At school!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'market' pronounced with an /m/ sound?" Expected answer: "Yes!"</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher also shows a picture of a school and asks, "What place is this?" (Expected: "School!").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short descriptive text (1-2 simple paragraphs) about a day at school, illustrated with pictures of different parts of the school (e.g., cafeteria, playground, library, nurse's office). The text is displayed on a projector or as a large printout.</p>
                <div style="background-color: #f9f9f9; border-left: 3px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; margin: 10px 0;">
                    <p style="margin: 0; font-style: italic;">"Hello! My name is Ana. I'm at school today. Our school is big and clean. I like to read books in the library. My friend Luis likes to play in the playground. The cafeteria is busy at lunchtime. The nurse's office is near the principal's office."</p>
                </div>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand and interpret short, simple written texts that describe familiar places and activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher reads the text aloud once, pointing to words as she reads. Then asks, "What is Ana talking about?" (Expected: "School!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> The teacher reads simple sentences based on the text. Students show thumbs up for True, thumbs down for False.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Ana is at home." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The school is big." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Luis plays in the library." (False - Luis plays in the playground)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The nurse's office is near the principal's office." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher provides a list of places (e.g., library, playground, cafeteria) and asks students to point to the corresponding picture in the text or on the board as they read the sentences again. "Where does Ana read books?" (Students point to 'library' picture).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading simple sentences using contractions ("I'm"), prepositions of place ("at", "in", "near", "next to"), present simple tense ("I'm at school."), and vocabulary for places (school, library, playground, cafeteria, nurse's office, principal's office, gym) and actions (read, play, study). Phonemic awareness: blending /ɪn/ for "in" and /bɪɡ/ for "big".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap-fill Reading:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple sentences about a school map, each with a missing preposition of place or a missing place name. Students read the sentences and fill in the blanks using a word bank. Example sentences: "I am ____ school." "The library is ____ the gym." "The students _____ books in the _____. " (Word bank: at, near, read, library). The teacher also includes words like "in" and "big" for phonemic awareness: "I am playing ___ the park." (Students fill 'in', then blend /ɪ-n/). "Our school is very ___." (Students fill 'big', then blend /b-ɪ-g/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Matching:</b> The teacher shows a simple map of a school with various rooms labeled (e.g., "cafeteria," "gym," "principal's office," "secretary's office"). On the board, the teacher writes sentences that describe locations (e.g., "The gym is next to the cafeteria." "The secretary's office is near the principal's office."). Students read each sentence and draw a line from the sentence to the correct place on their school map drawing or worksheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Find the Place":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small school map and a set of cards with written instructions like: "Find the library." "Point to the playground." "Where is the nurse's office?" "Walk to the cafeteria." One student reads an instruction card aloud, and the other student reads the map and points to the correct location. They switch roles after a few turns. The teacher monitors for correct pronunciation of /sk/ and /m/ and the use of prepositions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "School Tour - Following Instructions"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a simple, imagined "school layout" drawing or a large, simple map of a town with key places (school, park, library, market, hospital, store, home). The teacher prepares a set of "Tour Guide Instructions" on separate cards for each group. These instructions are simple, sequential written commands that guide students to "visit" different places on their map. Example instructions for a school map:
                <ul style="list-style-type: square; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Start at the principal's office."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Walk to the secretary's office. It's next to the principal's office."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Go to the library. Read a book there."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Now, find the playground. Play for a while."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Your tour ends at the cafeteria. It is busy!"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students take turns reading an instruction card aloud to their group. The other group members must follow the instruction by pointing to the correct location on their map. They confirm the location by saying, "I'm at the library!" or "We are at the playground!" and using polite expressions like "Excuse me, where is the gym?" "It's near the nurse's office. Thank you!" The goal is to successfully navigate the "tour" by correctly reading and interpreting the instructions. The teacher walks around, listening to their reading and interactions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist & Quick Quiz</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a short passage (2-3 sentences) describing a familiar place, such as their school or a park, and a simple multiple-choice question or a drawing task. Example passage: "My school has a big playground. I like to play there with my friends. We have a small library too. I can read many books."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Can identify the main idea or answer simple questions about the text (e.g., "What does the school have? a) a big hospital b) a big playground").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Written Instructions:</b> Can correctly point to or identify places on a simple map based on written commands.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Recognizes and understands words like "school," "playground," "library," "big," "small."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Demonstrates blending of sounds for words like "in" and "big" if asked to read them.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket by answering:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new place you read about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to follow the instructions?" (Students circle 'easy' or 'difficult').</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about places was most interesting for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you want to learn more about our school or other places?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., student engagement with the map activity, clarity of instructions, students' ability to identify main ideas).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction)? (e.g., more practice with specific prepositions, reviewing polite expressions, preparing clearer visual cues for dialogues). Perhaps the students struggled with reading longer sentences; I might shorten them for the next reading task or provide more pre-reading strategies.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading about places and following written instructions. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students verbally describe locations and give directions to each other using the places and prepositions learned, reinforcing the theme "I'm at School." and the scenario "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>